{"config_version":"cfg-07e2771c91cb","difficulty":"hard","engine_commit":"23520120c53ee78b5bdf00530dd982d18aba070c","episode_id":"p3-helpdesk__escalate_breached_enterprise_tickets__1","error":null,"final_reply":"Seven tickets met all four conditions (status open, priority high, enterprise customer, SLA breached), and each is now escalated with a reason that names the SLA breach:\n\n- tkt_0001 (cus_0001 Acme Logistics)\n- tkt_0033 (cus_0012 Lakeside Insurance)\n- tkt_0095 (cus_0020 Starling Pharma)\n- tkt_0145 (cus_0020 Starling Pharma)\n- tkt_0214 (cus_0004 Bluefin Freight)\n- tkt_0254 (cus_0012 Lakeside Insurance)\n- tkt_0293 (cus_0057 Beacon Travel)\n\nEach escalate call returned HTTP 200 with status \"escalated\" and escalation_level 1.\n\nHow I got there: I listed the 8 enterprise customers (the list had no further pages), then listed open, high-priority, SLA-breached tickets across both pages. I cross-referenced each ticket's customer_id against the enterprise list. The other breached tickets belong to standard or premium customers and were left alone.\n\nThe escalate action also reassigns each ticket to the agent on call at level 1, which changed assignee_id to agt_0003 on all seven. That is the action's built-in behaviour, not an extra change from me. I made no other writes.","final_state_hash":"3865b4f2a67e4c6b78cb78cf63dfab7cc997cde83dd70f4dfe834af32800f2d7","initial_state_hash":"caf18edc357ffd4bce7f2fd17dc6d27119efc76154c33267aea4f4ac90dd1ac9","messages":[{"role":"user","seq":0,"text":"Escalate every ticket that is in status open, has high priority, belongs to an enterprise customer and has breached its SLA. Give each escalation a reason that names the SLA breach. Change nothing else.","type":"instruction"},{"call_id":"c1","commentary":"","request":{"method":"GET","path":"/customers","query":{"limit":"25","tier":"enterprise"}},"role":"assistant","seq":1,"type":"tool_call"},{"body":{"data":[{"created_at":"2025-06-14T09:00:00.000Z","email":"support@acmelogis